n class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Judah was an ancient kingdom in the southern Levant with its capital at Jerusalem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/judaism"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/judaism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Judaism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Judaism is the monotheistic religion of the Jewish people, based on the Hebrew Bible and rabbinic tradition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/judar-pasha"><span class="enc-name">Judar Pasha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judar Pasha was a commander in the service of the Saadi sultan of Morocco, of Spanish origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/judas-maccabeus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Judas Maccabeus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judas Maccabeus was a Jewish priest and military leader, the third son of Mattathias, who led the revolt against the Seleucid king Antiochus IV from 166 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee"><span class="enc-name">Judas of Galilee</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judas of Galilee was a Jewish leader who led resistance to the census of Quirinius when Judaea became a Roman province in 6 CE, teaching that paying tax to Rome meant…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/judicael"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/judicael.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Judicael</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judicael was a king of Domnonée in northern Brittany in the 7th century, later venerated as a saint.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Judicial Committee of the Privy Council</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Judicial Committee of the Privy Council, set up in London in 1833, served as the final court of appeal for British colonies and dominions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/judicial-papyrus-of-turin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/judicial-papyrus-of-turin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Judicial Papyrus of Turin</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Judicial Papyrus of Turin is an Egyptian papyrus of the twelfth century BCE recording the trials of those accused in the harem conspiracy against Ramesses III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-flanders"><span class="enc-name">Judith of Flanders</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judith of Flanders was a daughter of Count Baldwin IV of Flanders who married Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria, and after his death in 1066 Welf IV, Duke of Bavaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/judith-of-france"><span class="enc-name">Judith of France</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Judith was a daughter of the Frankish king Charles the Bald who married two kings of Wessex, Æthelwulf in 856 and then his son Æthelbald.</span></a></li></ul>
